{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789333137,"nonce":"zQlpiqM5pwu4L4Uh9OwrC3Nv9fY=","title":"Python: a default on a nested def is not honoured","message":"def outer(k):\n        def f(x, y=k):\n            return x + y\n        return f\n    print(outer(10)(1))\n\nraises \"TypeError: function takes 2 positional arguments but 1 were\ngiven\"; CPython prints 11. A nested def is lowered to a record whose code\ntakes every parameter, and `zb_call_N` checks the arity against the full\ncount; the default is never evaluated. A default on a nested def would\nhave to be evaluated where the def is (Python evaluates it once, at\ndefinition time) and stored with the record, with the adapter filling\nmissing arguments from it.\n\nThe known-callee path (86da450) leaves such defs out of the closure table\nfor the same reason, so they take the record path and fail the same way.","files":null}]}